  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 350 AM CDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nSatellite and surface observations early this morning show a weak \nfrontal boundary bisecting northeastern Kansas and is expected to \nmove south a bit through the early morning hours today before \nstalling across eastern Kansas. Zooming out, a few notable features \nstand out on upper-level water vapor imagery that will act to drive \nour weather in eastern Kansas the next several days - one ejecting \noff the northern Mexican mountains into the southern Plains, and the \nother a strong PNW cyclone embedded in broad troughing over the \nwestern coast. Through the day today, the southern Plains \ndisturbance will continue its march north towards Kansas and \ncontinue to reinforce southerly low-level moisture advection towards \nthe stalled boundary in Kansas. Isentropic ascent will strengthen \novertop of the stalled warm front by the late afternoon and evening \nhours helping to generate precipitation. Cannot rule out a strong to \nmarginally severe storm near the surface boundary by late this \nafternoon given 1500-2000 J/kg of MUCAPE and 30-35 knots of bulk \nshear. That said, storms should be elevated so the main hazards \nwould be small hail and an isolated strong wind gust. Rain/storm \nchances will slowly shift north through the overnight hours as best \nlift associated with the nose of the LLJ slides into southern NE and \nsouthwestern IA.\n\nA brief lull in rain chances should be realized for much of Friday \nmorning and into the early afternoon hours before lift from the \nsouthern Plains disturbance moves into eastern Kansas by the later \nafternoon and evening. Widespread rain and isolated thunderstorms \nwill persist until early Saturday morning before large-scale ascent \nbegins to shift north and east followed quickly by widespread \nsubsidence. This should bring an end to precipitation Saturday \nmorning/early afternoon from west to east - good news for Trick or \nTreaters!\n\nBy Sunday, a compact ridge will over the central Plains will keep \nthings quiet, but the large amplified trough that begins to eject \noff the southern Rockies later that evening will not keep things \nquiet for long. WAA ahead of the main trough axis should begin the \nrain/storm chances Sunday night into Monday morning with \nprecipitation chances hanging around all day Monday into Tuesday. \nGiven the strength of this system, there is some potential in a \nsevere weather setup somewhere across the southern/central Plains \nMonday evening and possibly again Tuesday, but variability in model \nguidance at this time is still high keeping confidence low. The \nupper trough axis is progged to move east of the area Wednesday, \nushering in more seasonable and drier conditions in for the \nremainder of the week.",
